WEBVTT

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.713
Hi, I'm Strata!

00:00:02.713 --> 00:00:06.389
This is how a filesystem can keep a hundred snapshots without

00:00:06.389 --> 00:00:07.726
keeping a hundred copies.

00:00:09.433 --> 00:00:12.866
A snapshot is just a small tree of hashes, pointing at chunks

00:00:12.866 --> 00:00:14.010
the store already holds.

00:00:17.967 --> 00:00:20.676
An agent edits files in its workspace overlay.

00:00:20.676 --> 00:00:24.402
The chunker cuts files over a hundred and twenty-eight kibibytes where

00:00:24.402 --> 00:00:25.757
their content says to.

00:00:26.177 --> 00:00:27.890
Each chunk is hashed and compressed.

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:30.746
A chunk the store already has is not written again.

00:00:31.166 --> 00:00:33.752
Directories become pages of about sixty entries.

00:00:33.752 --> 00:00:36.708
A one file change rewrites one small page.

00:00:37.128 --> 00:00:38.659
The new root is the commit.

00:00:38.659 --> 00:00:41.211
It goes into the store, and nothing old is touched.

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:46.412
With flat directories, changing one file rewrites the whole listing.

00:00:46.832 --> 00:00:49.990
With pages, only the page holding that file is written again.

00:00:51.675 --> 00:00:55.341
In my toy, structure was most of what each late snapshot cost.

00:00:55.341 --> 00:00:57.479
The file content was the smaller part.

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:03.044
When you size a snapshot store, count payload and structure separately.

00:01:07.983 --> 00:01:11.155
Chunk by content, store each chunk once, and page the tree.

00:01:12.583 --> 00:01:14.719
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:14.719 --> 00:01:15.329
I'm Strata.

00:01:15.329 --> 00:01:15.634
Bye!
